window.BCA_UNIT_SUBTOPICS = {
  architecture: [
    [
      {title:"Boolean operators and truth tables",notes:"Boolean algebra treats each input as either 0 or 1. AND produces 1 only when every input is 1; OR produces 1 when at least one input is 1; NOT reverses a bit. XOR produces 1 when its two inputs differ. A truth table is the safest way to verify a rule because it checks every possible input row before the circuit is built.",example:"For A=1 and B=0, A XOR B=1; for A=1 and B=1, A XOR B=0.",exam:"Write the operator rule, then include every input combination and its output."},
      {title:"Sum of products and product of sums",notes:"A sum-of-products expression ORs together AND terms, such as AB + A'C. A product-of-sums expression ANDs together OR terms, such as (A+B)(A'+C). To build SOP from a truth table, write one minterm for each output-1 row. To build POS, write a maxterm for each output-0 row. K-maps then simplify the expression without changing its truth table.",example:"If output is 1 only for A=1,B=0, the minterm is AB'.",exam:"State whether you are grouping 1s for SOP or 0s for POS before simplifying."},
      {title:"Signed binary and two's complement",notes:"Unsigned n-bit values range from 0 to 2ⁿ−1. Two's-complement signed values use the leftmost bit as a negative place weight, giving a range from −2ⁿ⁻¹ to 2ⁿ⁻¹−1. To form a negative value, invert all bits of its positive form and add 1. The same binary adder can then add signed values; overflow occurs when the mathematical result is outside the representable range.",example:"In 8 bits, +5=00000101. Invert to 11111010 and add 1: −5=11111011.",exam:"State the bit width, show invert-plus-one, and check the signed range for overflow."},
      {title:"Binary fractions and fixed place values",notes:"Digits to the right of the binary point have weights 1/2, 1/4, 1/8 and so on. Add the weights whose bits are 1. Some decimal fractions repeat forever in binary, so a computer with a fixed number of fraction bits stores an approximation. This explains why a displayed decimal result may differ by a tiny rounding amount.",example:"0.101₂ = 1/2 + 0/4 + 1/8 = 0.625₁₀.",exam:"Write the fractional weights explicitly and mention rounding when a value cannot be represented exactly."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Combinational versus sequential circuits",notes:"A combinational circuit has no memory: its output is determined by the inputs present now. A sequential circuit stores a state, so its output can depend on earlier inputs as well as current ones. A clock often coordinates state updates. When a question asks you to classify a circuit, look for a feedback path or a storage element such as a latch or flip-flop.",example:"An adder is combinational; a counter is sequential because it remembers its current count.",exam:"Explain whether earlier input history can affect the present output."},
      {title:"Universal NAND and NOR gates",notes:"NAND and NOR are called universal because either gate type can be connected to implement NOT, AND and OR, and therefore any Boolean function. For example, feeding both inputs of a NAND from A gives NOT A. Universal-gate designs are useful when a circuit is built from one standard gate family, though the number of gates and delay still matter.",example:"A NAND inverter is Y=A NAND A=A'.",exam:"Show the repeated-input inverter, then derive one other basic gate."},
      {title:"Flip-flop behavior and state tables",notes:"A flip-flop stores one bit and changes it at a clock event. A D flip-flop copies D to Q at the active edge. A T flip-flop holds when T=0 and toggles when T=1. A JK flip-flop holds, resets, sets or toggles according to J and K; the SR form has set and reset inputs, with a forbidden or undefined combination in the basic implementation. Use a state table to show present state and next state.",example:"For a T flip-flop with Q=0 and T=1 at the active edge, the next Q is 1.",exam:"Name the active clock event and distinguish present state from next state."},
      {title:"Synchronous counters and ripple counters",notes:"A counter moves through a sequence of binary states. In a synchronous counter, all flip-flops share the clock, so state bits update together after the clock edge. In a ripple counter, one flip-flop output clocks the next, making the change ripple through stages. Ripple counters are simple but accumulate propagation delay; synchronous designs need more logic but support faster, more coordinated changes.",example:"A 3-bit up-counter cycles 000, 001, 010, 011, 100, 101, 110, 111, then returns to 000.",exam:"Compare how the clock reaches each stage and connect that to delay."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"CPU buses and the datapath",notes:"A datapath moves values among registers, the arithmetic-logic unit and memory interfaces. Address, data and control signals have different jobs: an address selects a location, data carries a value, and control signals specify actions such as read or write. A shared bus saves wiring but only one source may drive it at a time. Tracing a data movement diagram helps explain how an instruction is carried out.",example:"A memory read places an address in MAR, asserts a read control signal, then receives data in MDR.",exam:"Identify the source, destination and control action for each transfer."},
      {title:"Instruction fields: opcode and operands",notes:"A machine instruction is encoded as bits. The opcode identifies the operation, while operand fields identify registers, values or addresses. Some instructions include addressing-mode bits or an immediate constant. Instruction length and field sizes constrain how many operations, registers or addresses can be represented. Decode a small instruction by labelling each field before describing its effect.",example:"In a simple format [opcode | destination | source], ADD | R1 | R2 means add the source value into the destination register.",exam:"Separate what operation is requested from where its operands are found."},
      {title:"Immediate, direct, indirect and register addressing",notes:"Addressing modes describe how an instruction reaches an operand. Immediate mode contains the value in the instruction; register mode names a CPU register; direct mode gives the memory address; indirect mode uses a location or register that contains the effective address. These modes trade instruction size, flexibility and memory accesses. Be clear whether a number is an operand value or an address.",example:"If memory[200]=35, direct address 200 reads 35; immediate #200 uses the value 200 itself.",exam:"Trace the address once and distinguish effective address from data."},
      {title:"Stack frames and subroutine return",notes:"A stack follows last-in, first-out order. A procedure call can place the return address and saved values on the stack; the called routine can use space for parameters or local data. On return, saved information is restored and execution continues at the return address. The stack pointer marks the current top. Nested calls work because each call has its own stack frame.",example:"If procedure A calls B and B calls C, C returns to B first; then B returns to A.",exam:"Trace each push and pop in order and state where execution resumes."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Pipeline stages and ideal speedup",notes:"Pipelining divides instruction work into stages that operate on different instructions at the same time. The first result still waits for the pipeline to fill, but after that an ideal pipeline can complete roughly one instruction per clock. For k stages and n instructions, ideal cycles are k+n−1, assuming equal stage time and no hazards. It improves throughput more directly than the latency of one instruction.",example:"A 4-stage ideal pipeline running 6 instructions takes 4+6−1=9 cycles, rather than 24 stage-times without overlap.",exam:"Write the ideal assumptions before calculating cycles or speedup."},
      {title:"Data, control and structural hazards",notes:"A data hazard occurs when an instruction needs a value that an earlier instruction has not finished producing. A control hazard occurs when a branch makes the next instruction uncertain. A structural hazard occurs when two operations need the same hardware resource at once. A processor may wait, forward a value, duplicate a resource or predict a branch. Name the hazard and explain why the next work cannot safely proceed.",example:"If instruction 2 uses R1 written by instruction 1, it may need forwarding or a stall.",exam:"Identify the dependency first, then describe the remedy and any cost."},
      {title:"Cache mapping and replacement",notes:"A cache holds copies of memory blocks. Direct mapping assigns each block to one cache line; fully associative mapping allows a block in any line; set-associative mapping allows a block in one set. A tag identifies which memory block is present. When a set is full, a replacement policy chooses a line to evict. The mapping choice trades hardware complexity, speed and conflict misses.",example:"With four direct-mapped lines, block numbers 0, 4 and 8 all map to line 0 if the line index is block number modulo 4.",exam:"Separate the block number, line or set index, and tag when solving a mapping question."},
      {title:"Interrupt I/O and DMA transfer",notes:"Programmed I/O makes the CPU repeatedly check a device. Interrupt-driven I/O lets the device request attention when ready, so the CPU can do other work meanwhile. Direct memory access (DMA) transfers a block between a device and memory after the CPU sets up address, direction and length; an interrupt can report completion. DMA is especially useful for larger transfers, though setup and coordination still take time.",example:"For a disk block, the CPU configures DMA once, continues another task, then handles the completion interrupt.",exam:"Compare who moves each data item and when the CPU is interrupted."}
    ]
  ],
  evs: [
    [
      {title:"Carrying capacity and resource limits",notes:"Carrying capacity is the population or level of use an environment can support over time without losing the resources and processes it depends on. It is not a fixed number: water availability, technology, consumption and ecosystem health can change it. Sustainable planning therefore looks at rates of use and renewal, not only the total amount currently available.",example:"A village may use groundwater sustainably when annual pumping stays near recharge, but not when wells are deepened every year to replace a falling water table.",exam:"Explain the limit as a relationship between demand, renewal and environmental condition."},
      {title:"Groundwater, recharge and the water table",notes:"Groundwater occupies pores and fractures below the ground. Rain or surface water can recharge an aquifer, but recharge may be slow and differs by geology and land cover. The water table marks the upper surface of saturated ground. Pumping faster than recharge can lower it, increase pumping costs or dry nearby wells. Protecting recharge zones and using water efficiently reduce pressure.",example:"If a field is paved, less rain may soak into the soil, so nearby groundwater recharge can fall.",exam:"Trace rainfall → infiltration → storage → pumping, and identify where a proposed activity changes the path."},
      {title:"Soil, forests and ecosystem services",notes:"Soil forms slowly from rock, organic matter, water and living activity. It stores water and nutrients and supports crops, so erosion or contamination can take years to repair. Forests hold soil, provide habitat, store carbon and influence water movement. Resource use should include these less visible services as well as products such as timber or crops.",example:"Removing vegetation on a slope lets rain carry topsoil into a stream, reducing farm fertility and increasing siltation.",exam:"Name one direct product and one ecological service for the resource in the question."},
      {title:"Environmental impact assessment",notes:"An environmental impact assessment (EIA) studies likely effects of a proposed project before approval or construction. A careful process describes the project and local baseline, predicts impacts, compares alternatives, plans mitigation and monitors results. Public consultation helps identify effects that technical surveys can miss. An EIA informs a decision; it does not automatically mean a project is harmless or should proceed.",example:"For a road, assess habitat fragmentation and noise, compare routes, plan crossings and monitor wildlife after construction.",exam:"Organize an EIA answer as baseline, impacts, alternatives, mitigation, consultation and monitoring."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Ecological succession",notes:"Succession is the gradual change in the species and structure of an ecosystem over time. Primary succession begins where soil is absent, while secondary succession follows disturbance where soil remains. Early colonizers change conditions and may make it easier for later species to establish. Real ecosystems can follow different pathways depending on climate, disturbance and local species.",example:"After a fire, grasses and shrubs may return before mature trees, because soil and some seeds remain.",exam:"Distinguish primary and secondary succession by the starting conditions."},
      {title:"Nutrient cycles and decomposers",notes:"Unlike energy, matter is reused. Decomposers such as fungi and bacteria break down dead organisms and waste, releasing nutrients to soil or water. Plants absorb nutrients, animals obtain them through food, and decomposition returns them to the environment. Human activities such as fertilizer overuse can alter these cycles and move nutrients into rivers and lakes.",example:"Excess fertilizer washes from a field into a pond, where nutrient enrichment can trigger algal growth.",exam:"Draw a loop showing how a named nutrient moves through organisms and the environment."},
      {title:"Invasive species and keystone roles",notes:"An invasive species is introduced beyond its native range and spreads in ways that can disrupt local ecosystems. It may compete, prey on native species or change habitat. A keystone species has an effect on ecosystem structure that is unusually large relative to its abundance. These terms describe different ideas: one is about introduction and spread, the other about ecological influence.",example:"If a predator controls a dominant grazer, removing that predator may allow grazing pressure to reshape vegetation.",exam:"Define the term carefully and describe the ecological link, not only the species name."},
      {title:"Protected areas and habitat corridors",notes:"Protected areas conserve habitats by limiting damaging activities, but boundaries alone may not address every threat. Corridors connect separated habitat patches and can allow movement, feeding and breeding. Conservation planning also considers local communities, land rights and long-term monitoring. A useful answer explains which threat an approach addresses and what conditions make it effective.",example:"A wildlife corridor across a road can reconnect two forest patches and reduce isolation of populations.",exam:"Match each conservation method to the problem it is designed to reduce."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"The three pillars and Sustainable Development Goals",notes:"Sustainable development considers environmental protection, social wellbeing and economic activity together. The Sustainable Development Goals provide a shared set of goals on areas such as health, education, water, energy and responsible consumption. A project may support one goal while harming another, so good planning identifies trade-offs and checks who benefits.",example:"A solar pump can expand irrigation, but a water-use plan is needed so cheaper pumping does not exhaust an aquifer.",exam:"Name the connected dimensions and discuss at least one trade-off or safeguard."},
      {title:"Corporate responsibility and resource accounting",notes:"Businesses affect the environment through materials, energy, water, emissions, waste and supply chains. Measuring these inputs and outputs makes it possible to set targets, find losses and report progress. Responsibility also includes safe working conditions, community impacts and honest communication. A claim such as “green” is meaningful only when it can be supported by evidence.",example:"A factory tracks water use per product, repairs leaks and reports both the baseline and the reduction achieved.",exam:"Give a measurable action and explain how results would be checked."},
      {title:"EIA steps and public participation",notes:"A project assessment begins with screening and defining the study scope, then gathers baseline information and predicts likely impacts. It considers alternatives, proposes mitigation, invites affected people to respond and sets a monitoring plan. Participation improves the assessment when communities can understand information, raise concerns and see how responses affected the decision.",example:"Residents may identify a seasonal wetland that a short dry-season survey missed.",exam:"Include consultation as a real decision step, not a formality at the end."},
      {title:"India's climate missions as connected actions",notes:"The National Action Plan on Climate Change describes missions covering solar energy, energy efficiency, sustainable habitats, water, Himalayan ecosystems, Green India, sustainable agriculture and climate knowledge. They address linked systems rather than a single “climate” activity. For each mission, connect its focus to a practical action and explain the climate or resilience benefit.",example:"Efficient buildings lower electricity demand, which can reduce emissions while making indoor spaces more comfortable.",exam:"Recall the mission themes accurately and explain one in a concrete setting."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Particulate matter and common air pollutants",notes:"Air pollutants include particulate matter and gases such as sulfur dioxide, nitrogen oxides and carbon monoxide. Their effects depend on concentration, exposure duration and the people or ecosystems exposed. Fine particles can travel deep into the lungs. Controls include cleaner fuels, emission standards, dust suppression and public transport, alongside monitoring to identify high-risk locations.",example:"Construction dust is a particulate source; covering material and wetting surfaces can reduce it at the source.",exam:"Name the pollutant, source, exposure route, effect and matching control."},
      {title:"Water pollution and treatment stages",notes:"Water may be polluted by sewage, industrial discharge, farm runoff, oil or solid waste. Treatment commonly removes large solids first, allows settling, uses biological processes to break down organic matter, and may disinfect water before release or reuse. The exact process depends on the pollutant and intended use. Preventing contamination upstream is often cheaper than treating it later.",example:"A sewage plant can screen solids, settle suspended matter, biologically treat organic waste and disinfect the effluent.",exam:"Connect the pollution source to a treatment step that can actually remove it."},
      {title:"Hazardous, electronic and biodegradable waste",notes:"Different waste streams need different handling. Food and garden waste can often be composted; electronic waste may contain recoverable metals and hazardous substances; biomedical and chemical waste require controlled collection and treatment. Mixing them can expose workers and pollute soil or water. Source separation makes safe recovery and disposal possible.",example:"A used battery should go to a suitable collection point, not into a compost bin or ordinary open dump.",exam:"Classify the waste first, then state the safe route and reason."},
      {title:"Risk, vulnerability and disaster preparedness",notes:"A hazard becomes a disaster when it affects exposed people or assets that cannot cope easily. Risk reduction lowers exposure or vulnerability before an event. Preparedness includes alerts, evacuation routes, trained people and supplies; response addresses immediate needs; recovery restores services and should reduce future risk. Businesses need plans for staff, buildings, data and supply interruptions.",example:"A flood warning system reduces risk only if people know the route and can safely reach higher ground.",exam:"Separate hazard, exposure, vulnerability and capacity in your explanation."}
    ]
  ],
  english: [
    [
      {title:"Denotation, connotation and register",notes:"Denotation is a word's basic dictionary meaning. Connotation is the feeling or association it carries. Register is the level of formality suited to a situation. Two words can point to a similar idea but create different effects, so choose a word by checking meaning, tone and audience together.",example:"“Childlike” often sounds innocent, while “childish” often criticizes immature behavior.",exam:"Explain the difference in meaning and tone using a sentence for each word."},
      {title:"Word families and parts of speech",notes:"A word family shares a base but may include different parts of speech. A noun names something, a verb expresses an action or state, an adjective describes a noun and an adverb often describes a verb or adjective. Recognizing the required slot in a sentence helps you choose the right form instead of guessing from sound.",example:"Decide (verb), decision (noun), decisive (adjective), decisively (adverb).",exam:"Identify the sentence slot, then choose the word form that fits it."},
      {title:"Collocations and natural word partners",notes:"A collocation is a common pairing of words, such as make a decision or heavy rain. A phrase may be grammatically possible but sound unnatural if its word partners are unusual. Reading examples and recording a phrase with a new word helps you learn how it is actually used.",example:"We say “strong tea” more naturally than “powerful tea.”",exam:"Give a complete natural phrase, not only a one-word definition."},
      {title:"Spaced vocabulary recall",notes:"Vocabulary sticks when you retrieve it repeatedly over time. Write the word, meaning and your own sentence, then cover the meaning and try to recall it later. Mix old and new words rather than rereading one list passively. If recall fails, check the meaning and try another sentence.",example:"Review a new word today, tomorrow and again several days later, each time making a fresh sentence.",exam:"Show the word in a context that proves you know its meaning."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Subject, verb and complete thought",notes:"A complete independent clause has a subject and a verb and expresses a thought that can stand alone. The subject tells who or what the clause is about; the verb states an action or condition. Long sentences can hide the subject, so locate the main verb and ask who or what performs it before checking punctuation.",example:"In “The students in the lab are preparing a report,” students is the subject and are preparing is the verb.",exam:"Underline the subject and main verb before repairing a fragment."},
      {title:"Run-ons, comma splices and sentence boundaries",notes:"A run-on joins independent clauses without correct punctuation. A comma splice joins them using only a comma. Repair either by making two sentences, using a semicolon when ideas are closely related, or adding a suitable conjunction. Punctuation should show how ideas relate rather than simply where a speaker pauses.",example:"Incorrect: “The file was missing, we searched again.” Correct: “The file was missing, so we searched again.”",exam:"Find both independent clauses and choose a boundary that fits their relationship."},
      {title:"Transitions that show relationships",notes:"Transition words signal how ideas connect. “Because” introduces a reason, “therefore” signals a result, “however” signals contrast and “for example” introduces evidence. A transition cannot fix an illogical order by itself; first decide the relationship, then choose a word that accurately names it.",example:"“The first draft was short; however, the revised draft included evidence.”",exam:"Name the relationship between sentences before selecting a transition."},
      {title:"Proofreading one layer at a time",notes:"Editing is easier when you check one kind of issue at a time. First confirm meaning and paragraph order, then sentence completeness, agreement and tense, and finally spelling and punctuation. Reading aloud can reveal missing words or awkward rhythm, while reading backward word by word helps isolate spelling errors.",example:"On a second pass, check every verb against its subject instead of trying to fix everything at once.",exam:"Describe a specific revision and explain how it improves clarity or correctness."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Subject–verb agreement with tricky subjects",notes:"A verb agrees with its subject, not with a nearby noun. Phrases between them can distract: “The list of topics is long” uses is because list is singular. Indefinite pronouns and compound subjects also affect agreement. Find the real subject first, then choose the verb form.",example:"“A box of old books was found,” because box is the subject.",exam:"Mark the subject and ignore intervening phrases before choosing the verb."},
      {title:"Pronoun antecedents and point of view",notes:"A pronoun should clearly refer to a noun called its antecedent and should agree with it in number and meaning. If a sentence contains two possible nouns, repeat the noun rather than leave the reader guessing. Keep point of view consistent unless there is a clear reason to change it.",example:"Unclear: “When Riya spoke to Nita, she was worried.” Name which person was worried.",exam:"Replace an unclear pronoun with the exact noun it means."},
      {title:"Articles and countability",notes:"Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound; the choice follows sound, not just spelling. Use the when the reader can identify a particular item. Some nouns are countable and take a/an or plural forms; others are usually uncountable, such as information. Article errors often come from translating directly from another language.",example:"“An hour” begins with a vowel sound; “a university” begins with a consonant y-sound.",exam:"Check sound, specificity and whether the noun is countable."},
      {title:"Tense consistency and prepositions",notes:"Verb tense shows when an action happens. Keep the timeline consistent unless the meaning changes time, such as moving from past events to a present result. Prepositions form common patterns with verbs and adjectives, so learn them in phrases and check whether a time or place relationship is intended.",example:"“She has worked here since 2022” uses since for the starting point; “for four years” gives duration.",exam:"State the intended time, then check the verb form and its natural preposition."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Descriptive writing with selected detail",notes:"Description helps a reader picture a person, place, object or event. Choose a few precise details that support the main impression instead of listing every visible thing. Sensory words can help, but each detail should serve a purpose and the sequence should guide the reader through the scene.",example:"Describe a busy station through the echoing announcement, the moving queue and the bright departure board.",exam:"Use precise details and an order such as near-to-far or first-to-last."},
      {title:"Definition writing: class and difference",notes:"A clear definition often names the general class first, then explains the features that distinguish the term from others in that class. Avoid defining a word by repeating it. If the concept is technical, give a plain-language explanation and one boundary or example so the reader knows what belongs and what does not.",example:"A compiler is a program that translates source code into another form, often machine code, before execution.",exam:"State the class, defining feature and a short example or contrast."},
      {title:"Classification and parallel categories",notes:"Classification groups items using one clear basis, such as purpose, size or process. Categories should not overlap confusingly and should be parallel: if one category describes function, the others should also describe function. State the organizing rule before listing groups and use examples to show where items belong.",example:"Classify communication by direction: one-way, two-way or group communication.",exam:"Name the basis of classification and make the categories consistent."},
      {title:"Essay structure and paragraph unity",notes:"An introduction gives context and a focused central claim. Each body paragraph develops one supporting point with explanation and evidence. A conclusion brings the argument together and shows why it matters; it should not simply repeat every sentence. Planning a short outline prevents a promising introduction from leading to unrelated paragraphs.",example:"For an essay on reading, plan one paragraph on vocabulary, one on knowledge and one on concentration.",exam:"Make each paragraph support the central claim and use transitions between points."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Comprehension: main idea and inference",notes:"The main idea is the central point a passage develops. Details support it with examples, reasons or evidence. An inference is a conclusion supported by clues in the passage, even when it is not stated word for word. Separate what the text says from what you infer, and point to the clue that supports the inference.",example:"If a passage says the sky darkened and people hurried indoors, you can infer rain may be coming, but the passage has not stated that it rained.",exam:"Answer from the passage and cite the sentence or clue that supports your response."},
      {title:"Summary versus précis",notes:"A summary briefly restates the main points of a passage. A précis is a compact, coherent account that preserves the central argument and logical order, usually in your own words and within a required length. Neither should add your opinion or copy long phrases. Keep essential ideas and remove repetition and minor examples.",example:"A 300-word passage reduced to about one-third length should retain its claim and reasons, not every illustration.",exam:"Follow the length rule given by the question and preserve the writer's meaning."},
      {title:"A repeatable précis workflow",notes:"Read the passage once for its overall idea and again to mark the main claim and supporting points. Draft a title, remove examples that are not essential, connect the remaining points in the original logical order and count words. Revise for accuracy, grammar and proportion. A short précis still needs a beginning, connected middle and complete ending.",example:"If a paragraph repeats a benefit three ways, keep the core benefit and omit the repeated phrasing.",exam:"Show a concise, neutral result that keeps the passage's argument intact."},
      {title:"Planning an essay under time pressure",notes:"Before writing, translate the prompt into a task, choose a clear position or purpose and make a brief outline. Estimate time for planning, drafting and a final check. Each body point should have a reason and a supporting example. Leave a few minutes to confirm that you answered the actual question and completed the ending.",example:"For a 30-minute answer, spend a few minutes outlining, most of the time drafting and the final minutes checking.",exam:"Use the prompt's key terms in your plan so no required part is missed."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Articulation and clear pronunciation",notes:"Articulation is how speech sounds are formed by the tongue, lips, teeth, palate and airflow. Clear speech does not require copying another accent; it requires producing sounds clearly enough for the listener to understand. Practice difficult sound contrasts slowly, then place them in words and sentences at a natural pace.",example:"Say “ship” and “sip” slowly, notice the vowel difference, then practise both in a short sentence.",exam:"Explain pronunciation as clear sound production and give a practice method."},
      {title:"Word stress and sentence focus",notes:"Word stress gives one syllable greater prominence, while sentence stress highlights the words that carry the important message. A change in stress can change which part of a sentence the speaker corrects or emphasizes. Use a dictionary or listening model for unfamiliar word stress and avoid stressing every word equally.",example:"“I asked for the BLUE file” emphasizes the file color; “I asked for the blue FILE” may contrast it with another object.",exam:"Mark the stressed syllable or word and explain how emphasis affects meaning."},
      {title:"Intonation and conversational meaning",notes:"Intonation is the rise and fall of pitch across a phrase. It can signal a question, completion, uncertainty, politeness or contrast, depending on language and context. The same words can sound like a genuine question or a sarcastic comment when pitch changes. Practise by listening to a speaker and marking where pitch rises or falls.",example:"A rising pitch at the end can make “You finished?” sound like a question.",exam:"Describe the pitch movement and its likely communicative effect."},
      {title:"Active listening and turn-taking",notes:"Good conversation involves listening for the speaker's main point, giving relevant responses and allowing balanced turns. Clarifying questions check understanding; paraphrasing shows what you heard. Avoid interrupting or planning a reply so early that you miss the speaker's meaning. In a group, invite quieter participants and keep comments connected to the discussion.",example:"“So your main concern is timing; have I understood you correctly?” checks understanding before replying.",exam:"Name a listening behavior and explain how it supports communication."}
    ]
  ],
  iks: [
    [
      {title:"Oral, written and teacher–student transmission",notes:"Knowledge can be preserved and shared through oral recitation, written texts, teaching lineages, debate and practical training. Each method has strengths and limits: memorized recitation can preserve wording, while a manuscript can be copied, annotated or damaged. Studying how knowledge travelled helps explain why texts, teachers and institutions all matter in a knowledge tradition.",example:"A learner may study a text with a teacher, practise its recitation and discuss how a passage should be interpreted.",exam:"Name the method of transmission and explain what it contributes."},
      {title:"The six Vedangas as supporting disciplines",notes:"The Vedangas support the study and use of Vedic texts: Shiksha concerns sound, Kalpa procedures, Vyakarana grammar, Nirukta word explanation, Chandas metre and Jyotisha calendrical timing. They are complementary tools rather than six versions of the same subject. Linking each name to its task makes the list easier to remember and explain.",example:"Chandas helps identify a verse's metre; Shiksha guides its pronunciation.",exam:"Write all six names and pair each with its distinct purpose."},
      {title:"Darshanas and different philosophical questions",notes:"The syllabus names six Vedic schools—Samkhya, Yoga, Nyaya, Vaisheshika, Mimamsa and Vedanta—and non-Vedic traditions such as Charvaka, Buddhist and Jain thought. They do not all make the same claims. Compare them through questions such as what counts as knowledge, what is real, how action works and what liberation means.",example:"Nyaya emphasizes reasoning and sources of knowledge; Yoga sets out a disciplined path of practice and insight.",exam:"Name the traditions accurately and compare one question they answer differently."},
      {title:"Itihasa, Purana and niti are distinct genres",notes:"Itihasa, Puranic literature and niti literature have different purposes and forms. The Ramayana and Mahabharata are commonly treated as Itihasa in the course; Puranas include narrative and cosmological material; niti texts discuss conduct, counsel or practical wisdom. Treat genre as a clue to how a text organizes and communicates knowledge.",example:"A story may illustrate a question of duty, while a niti passage may state a practical principle more directly.",exam:"Define each genre in the course's terms and give a named example."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Panini's rule-based grammar",notes:"Panini's Ashtadhyayi organizes Sanskrit grammar through concise rules and technical devices. Rules apply under stated conditions and may interact with other rules, so the system must be read as a structured method rather than a list of isolated observations. It is a useful example of how language can be described through formal, repeatable procedures.",example:"A grammar rule can specify how a sound or form changes when a particular suffix is added.",exam:"Explain the rule-based method and why context and ordering matter."},
      {title:"Place value and the role of zero",notes:"In a place-value system, a digit's position determines its weight. Zero can act as a placeholder that keeps positions distinct and can also represent a quantity. This makes writing and calculating with large numbers more systematic. When discussing the history, distinguish a numeral's written form, its use as a placeholder and its mathematical role.",example:"In 205, the zero shows there are no tens; without it, 205 and 25 would be confused.",exam:"Use a number example and explain exactly what the zero is doing."},
      {title:"Bhuta-Sankhya and Katapayadi encoding",notes:"Bhuta-Sankhya uses familiar objects or ideas associated with numbers as a way to encode values in words. Katapayadi assigns numerical values to selected consonants so that a phrase can encode a number; reading conventions determine the final order. These are historical encoding methods, so show the mapping supplied by the course before decoding a sample.",example:"If a class table maps a particular consonant to 3, use that stated table rather than guessing a value from memory.",exam:"Write the encoding rule, show each symbol's value and state any reversal convention."},
      {title:"Pramana, prameya and samshaya",notes:"In Indian epistemological discussions, pramana is a means or source of knowledge, prameya is the object known, and samshaya is doubt or uncertainty about a matter. These terms help structure inquiry: what is being examined, by what means and whether the conclusion is certain. Explain them as related parts of reasoning rather than isolated translations.",example:"For a claim about a distant object, identify the object of knowledge and ask what valid evidence supports the claim.",exam:"Define each term and connect it to a small example of inquiry."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Sulba geometry and construction",notes:"Sulba Sutras describe practical geometric procedures associated with altar construction. Their rules connect measured lengths, shapes and area, showing mathematics applied to a defined task. When studying a historical construction rule, state the context and explain the geometric relationship rather than presenting it as a modern proof unless the syllabus does so.",example:"A builder can use measured lengths and a diagonal relationship to construct a right-angled layout.",exam:"Name the practical context, describe the construction and show the geometric idea."},
      {title:"Pingala's prosody and binary-like patterns",notes:"Pingala's work on Sanskrit metre examines patterns made from short and long syllables. A verse pattern can be represented as a sequence of two choices, which creates links to counting and combinatorial thinking. Historical comparison should be careful: explain the pattern the syllabus teaches without claiming that it is identical to modern computer binary arithmetic.",example:"For three syllable positions with two choices each, there are 2×2×2=8 possible patterns.",exam:"List patterns in a consistent order and explain the counting rule."},
      {title:"Panchanga: five calendar measures",notes:"A Panchanga combines tithi, vara, nakshatra, yoga and karana. These describe different calendrical measures, including lunar day, weekday, lunar mansion and calculated relationships. Together they organize time using several astronomical conventions. Memorize the five names with a short meaning for each, and follow the definitions used in your course.",example:"A date entry can include both a weekday and a tithi because they describe different measures.",exam:"Name and define all five limbs; do not collapse them into a single “date” value."},
      {title:"Jantar Mantar and observational instruments",notes:"Jantar Mantar observatories contain large instruments designed for observing and calculating astronomical positions and time. Their scale and geometry made certain observations possible, while their accuracy depended on construction and use. Describe a named instrument from class, what it measured and how an observer interpreted the result.",example:"An instrument aligned with a celestial direction can help estimate a body's apparent position or local time.",exam:"Connect the instrument's structure to the observation it was designed to support."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Metallurgy as a sequence of craft decisions",notes:"Metalworking involves selecting and preparing ore or metal, heating it, smelting or refining it, shaping it and finishing the object. Fuel, furnace design, temperature, alloy composition and tool skill influence the result. Historical evidence may include objects, furnaces, slag and texts; use the evidence named in class rather than making a broad claim without support.",example:"A blacksmith heats iron until workable, hammers it to shape, then cools and finishes the tool.",exam:"Describe process steps in order and connect one material choice to the product's properties."},
      {title:"Iron and steel: material properties and process",notes:"Iron and steel differ in composition and properties; steel contains controlled carbon and may include other alloying elements. Historical iron production required knowledge of ore, furnace conditions and working methods. Avoid treating every ancient iron object as the same material. Name the example or period from your course and explain what the process reveals about technology.",example:"Changing carbon content can make a steel tool harder or more workable, depending on treatment.",exam:"Distinguish iron from steel and support historical points with the course example."},
      {title:"Dyes, pigments and painting surfaces",notes:"Color-making may use soluble dyes, which interact with fibres, or pigments, which are particles applied with a binder. A process can involve preparing material, treating a surface, applying color and fixing or sealing it. The final result depends on material, technique and the surface. Explain a specific craft method rather than saying only that ancient people used natural colors.",example:"A mordant can help some dyes bind to cloth fibres and improve color retention.",exam:"Name the material, preparation, application and reason for any treatment."},
      {title:"Town planning and temple architecture",notes:"Built environments show how people organized movement, water, work, worship and community life. Town planning may include streets, drainage and building zones; temple forms may include a sanctum, halls, towers or gateways, with regional variation. Vastu Shastra is a historical body of architectural ideas and texts. Describe context and evidence; do not treat every historical prescription as a modern engineering test.",example:"A drainage layout is both a design feature and evidence about how a settlement managed water.",exam:"Name the architectural feature and explain its practical or cultural role."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Daily routines, food, water and sleep",notes:"The syllabus discusses wellbeing through habits such as food, water, sleep and daily routine. Describe a traditional framework in its own terms and separate it from present-day clinical advice. For a student, a concrete learning point is that regular rest, hydration and balanced routines support attention and consistent study, while individual health needs differ.",example:"A study plan that includes sleep and breaks is more sustainable than an all-night revision schedule.",exam:"Explain the framework respectfully and use careful, evidence-aware wording."},
      {title:"Yoga paths and practices",notes:"Yoga includes philosophical ideas and several kinds of practice, not only physical postures. Depending on the syllabus, distinguish ethical discipline, meditation, knowledge, action or devotion from physical techniques. Explain the practice's purpose within its tradition and avoid treating one modern class format as the entire subject.",example:"A breathing or meditation practice can be discussed as attention training within a wider path of disciplined practice.",exam:"Name the practice, its context and how it relates to the broader idea of yoga."},
      {title:"Triguna as a contextual framework",notes:"Triguna describes three qualities: sattva is associated with clarity or balance, rajas with activity or desire, and tamas with inertia or obscurity. The framework is used in particular Indian philosophical and cultural traditions. It should not be treated as a measured modern diagnosis or a fixed label for a person's character.",example:"A passage describing calm clarity may be interpreted through sattva, while a restless desire for action may be discussed through rajas.",exam:"Define the three terms and state the tradition or context in which they are used."},
      {title:"Dharma, Arthashastra and governance",notes:"The Ramayana can be studied for narratives about duty and ethical choice; the Arthashastra discusses statecraft through administration, revenue, law, diplomacy and security. They are different texts with different aims. Historical prescriptions should be explained in context rather than equated directly with present constitutional systems or democratic institutions.",example:"Compare a narrative ethical dilemma with a practical administrative rule and identify the different purpose of each text.",exam:"Name the text, its concern and one contextual limitation of a modern comparison."}
    ]
  ],
  math: [
    [
      {title:"Set identities and complements",notes:"Set operations follow useful identities: union and intersection are commutative and associative, while distributive laws connect them. De Morgan's laws say the complement of a union is the intersection of the complements, and vice versa. A Venn diagram makes these relationships visible; an element-by-element argument can prove them precisely.",example:"If x is not in A∪B, then x is in neither A nor B, so x is in A'∩B'.",exam:"State the universe and use either a Venn diagram or an element argument."},
      {title:"Counting Cartesian-product pairs",notes:"The Cartesian product A×B contains all ordered pairs whose first element comes from A and second from B. Because the order matters, (a,b) and (b,a) are generally different. If A has m elements and B has n, the product has mn pairs. A small grid prevents missing or repeating pairs.",example:"A={1,2}, B={x,y}: A×B={(1,x),(1,y),(2,x),(2,y)}.",exam:"List pairs in a consistent order and check the count with |A×B|=|A||B|."},
      {title:"Relation matrices and digraphs",notes:"A relation on a finite set can be shown as ordered pairs, a Boolean matrix or a directed graph. In its matrix, entry (i,j) is 1 when the relation includes (i,j). A directed edge i→j represents the same fact. Choose the representation that makes the property you need to test easiest to see.",example:"For R={(1,1),(1,2),(2,2)}, draw loops at 1 and 2 and an arrow 1→2.",exam:"State the element order used for rows and columns so the matrix is unambiguous."},
      {title:"Function mappings and inverse functions",notes:"A mapping diagram shows whether each domain element has exactly one arrow to the codomain. A function has an inverse function only when it is one-to-one and onto on the stated domain and codomain. To find an inverse, write y=f(x), solve for x, then exchange the variable names and check the domain restrictions.",example:"For f(x)=2x+3 on the reals, y=2x+3 gives x=(y−3)/2, so f⁻¹(x)=(x−3)/2.",exam:"State the domain and codomain before claiming an inverse exists."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Addition and multiplication rules of counting",notes:"Use the addition rule when choosing one of disjoint alternatives: if a task can be completed in m ways by route A or n ways by route B, there are m+n choices. Use the multiplication rule for stages that all occur: m choices followed by n choices give mn outcomes. Decide whether the choices are alternatives or successive steps before calculating.",example:"Choose one of 3 buses or 2 trains: 5 options. Choose a bus and one of 2 seats: 3×2 combinations.",exam:"Explain why addition or multiplication fits the structure of the choice."},
      {title:"Permutations with repeated objects",notes:"The formula nPr counts ordered selections of r distinct objects from n. If some objects repeat, dividing by the factorial of each repeated count removes duplicate arrangements. Always decide whether objects are distinct and whether repeated items should be treated as identical; the answer depends on that interpretation.",example:"The distinct arrangements of AAB are 3!/2!=3: AAB, ABA, BAA.",exam:"State whether order matters and account for repeated objects."},
      {title:"Binomial coefficients and Pascal's triangle",notes:"The coefficient nCr counts how many ways to choose r items from n and also appears in the expansion of (x+y)ⁿ. Pascal's triangle builds each interior value by adding the two above it, which follows from splitting choices into those that include one fixed item and those that do not. This gives a fast check on expansion coefficients.",example:"The row for n=3 is 1,3,3,1, so (x+y)³=x³+3x²y+3xy²+y³.",exam:"Match the power of x and y to the chosen term and verify coefficients sum to 2ⁿ."},
      {title:"Characteristic equations for recurrences",notes:"For a linear recurrence with constant coefficients, try a solution of the form rⁿ. Substitution produces a characteristic equation. Distinct roots give a sum of root powers; repeated roots require an additional factor such as n. Initial values determine the unknown constants. Check the result by computing the first terms of the original recurrence.",example:"For aₙ=3aₙ₋₁−2aₙ₋₂, r²−3r+2=0 has roots 1 and 2, so aₙ=C₁+C₂·2ⁿ.",exam:"Show the characteristic polynomial, roots, general form and use of initial conditions."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"The handshaking lemma and degree",notes:"In a finite undirected graph, the sum of all vertex degrees is 2|E| because every edge touches two ends. This means the number of odd-degree vertices must be even. The result provides a quick consistency check for a diagram and helps decide whether an Euler trail or circuit can exist.",example:"If degrees are 3, 2, 2 and 1, their sum is 8, so the graph has 4 edges.",exam:"Count each loop according to the course convention and check the degree sum."},
      {title:"Euler trails and circuits",notes:"An Euler trail uses every edge exactly once; an Euler circuit also returns to its starting vertex. In a connected undirected graph, an Euler circuit requires every vertex to have even degree; an open Euler trail requires exactly two odd-degree vertices. These conditions concern edges, not whether every vertex is visited once.",example:"A connected graph with degrees 2, 2, 2 and 4 can have an Euler circuit.",exam:"Check connectivity and count odd-degree vertices before naming the route."},
      {title:"Hamilton paths and cycles",notes:"A Hamilton path visits every vertex exactly once; a Hamilton cycle returns to the start after visiting every vertex once. Unlike Euler problems, Hamilton problems do not require using every edge. There is no single simple degree test that decides every graph, so a careful route construction or proof of impossibility is usually needed.",example:"A route A→B→D→C visits four vertices once, even if some graph edges remain unused.",exam:"Say explicitly whether the condition is about every vertex or every edge."},
      {title:"Planar graphs and faces",notes:"A planar graph can be drawn without edges crossing except at vertices. For a connected planar graph, Euler's formula is V−E+F=2, where F includes the outside face. The formula can help find a missing value, but only after confirming the graph is connected and planar. A drawing with crossings does not prove nonplanarity; it may be possible to redraw it.",example:"If V=6 and E=9 in a connected planar drawing, F=2−6+9=5.",exam:"Define the outside face and state the assumptions before applying the formula."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Determinants and singular matrices",notes:"The determinant summarizes important information about a square matrix. If det(A) is nonzero, the matrix is invertible and its rows or columns are independent; if det(A)=0, it is singular and has no inverse. For larger matrices, expansion by a row or column uses signed minors; choose a row with zeros when possible to reduce work.",example:"For [[2,1],[4,2]], det=2·2−1·4=0, so the matrix is singular.",exam:"Show the determinant calculation before deciding whether an inverse or Cramer's rule applies."},
      {title:"Row reduction and rank",notes:"Elementary row operations preserve the solution structure and lead toward echelon form. Swap rows, multiply a row by a nonzero constant or add a multiple of one row to another. The rank is the number of pivots or nonzero rows in echelon form. A zero row signals dependent information, not necessarily that the system has no solution.",example:"If reduction produces two pivots and one zero row, the matrix rank is 2.",exam:"Record each row operation clearly and count pivots, not original nonzero rows."},
      {title:"Eigenvalues and eigenvectors",notes:"An eigenvector is a nonzero direction that a square matrix scales without changing its direction. The scale factor is the eigenvalue λ, found by solving det(A−λI)=0. For each root, solve (A−λI)v=0 to find the eigenvectors. Eigenvectors may be multiplied by any nonzero constant, so give a valid nonzero vector and verify the equation.",example:"For A=[[2,0],[0,3]], eigenvalues are 2 and 3 with eigenvectors along the first and second coordinate axes.",exam:"Find λ first, then solve the homogeneous system and substitute to verify."},
      {title:"Using Cayley–Hamilton to reduce powers",notes:"Cayley–Hamilton states that every square matrix satisfies its own characteristic polynomial. If p(λ)=λ²−3λ+2 for a matrix A, then A²−3A+2I=0. Rearranging can reduce higher powers or, when the constant term is nonzero, produce an inverse relation. Keep the identity matrix visible; scalar terms become multiples of I.",example:"From A²−3A+2I=0, infer A²=3A−2I.",exam:"Write the characteristic polynomial, replace λ with A and keep scalar terms attached to I."}
    ]
  ],
  'problem-solving': [
    [
      {title:"Input, output and boundary cases",notes:"Before planning a solution, write exactly what input is valid, what result is required and any size or range limits. Boundary cases sit at the edges of those rules, such as an empty list, zero, a negative value or the largest allowed size. Handling them deliberately prevents a correct-looking algorithm from failing on an important case.",example:"For an average, zero inputs must be handled before dividing by the count.",exam:"List normal, smallest, largest and unusual inputs before describing the algorithm."},
      {title:"Correctness, termination and feasibility",notes:"A useful algorithm must do more than give the right result once. Correctness means it works for every valid input; termination means it eventually stops; feasibility means its time and memory are practical for the input size. A loop should show progress toward stopping, and the final answer should follow from the stated method.",example:"A loop that increments i toward n terminates if i increases each pass and n is finite.",exam:"State why the method is correct and what condition guarantees it ends."},
      {title:"Flowchart symbols and control flow",notes:"A flowchart uses an oval for start/end, a parallelogram for input/output, a rectangle for processing and a diamond for a decision. Arrows show the direction of control. Every decision should label both outcomes; every loop should have a clear path back to the condition and a route that eventually leaves the loop.",example:"A “number > 0?” diamond branches to “positive” on yes and “zero or negative” on no.",exam:"Use standard symbols and label the yes/no paths so the algorithm can be traced."},
      {title:"Test cases, traces and complexity",notes:"A dry run records variable values after each step. A test set should include ordinary cases, boundaries and cases that exercise different branches. After correctness, estimate how work grows with input size: one pass over n items is linear, while checking every pair is quadratic. These estimates help compare solutions as data grows.",example:"For n=100, a nested loop over all pairs may do roughly 10,000 checks, unlike a single pass of about 100.",exam:"Show input, expected result, branch or trace, and the reason the test matters."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Sequence, selection and repetition",notes:"Sequence executes steps in order. Selection chooses among paths based on a condition. Repetition performs a block again while a rule holds. These three structures form the basis of structured programming. A clear program keeps each block focused and avoids jumps that make the path difficult to follow.",example:"Read a score, select pass or fail, then repeat the same process for each student.",exam:"Identify the structure used and state how control moves through it."},
      {title:"if/else versus switch",notes:"Use if/else for ranges, compound conditions or comparisons. Use switch when one expression is compared with several discrete constant cases. In C, each switch case usually needs break to prevent control from falling through to the next case; default handles values not listed. Choose the form that makes the decision clearest.",example:"A numeric menu choice 1–4 suits switch; a score >= 40 suits if/else.",exam:"State whether the cases are exact values or broader conditions."},
      {title:"while and do-while loops",notes:"A while loop checks its condition before executing the body, so it can run zero times. A do-while loop checks after the body and therefore runs at least once. In C, the do-while ends with a semicolon. Choose based on whether the task must happen once before the condition can be checked.",example:"A menu should usually display once, so a do-while loop can be a natural fit.",exam:"Draw the condition check position and say whether zero iterations are possible."},
      {title:"Sentinels and loop safety",notes:"A sentinel is a special input that signals the end of a sequence. It should not be treated as ordinary data, and it must be distinct from valid values. Initialize variables before the loop, read input at the right point, and ensure every pass updates state. These checks prevent off-by-one errors and accidental infinite loops.",example:"Read temperatures until −999 appears; update the average only for values other than −999.",exam:"Explain why the sentinel cannot be confused with valid input and where it is checked."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Extract digits with remainder and division",notes:"For a nonnegative integer, n%10 returns the last decimal digit and integer division n/10 removes it. Repeating these operations processes the number from right to left. Save the original value before modifying n if a later comparison needs it. Take care with zero and negative values because their handling depends on the problem's definition.",example:"For 472: 472%10=2; then 472/10=47; next digits are 7 and 4.",exam:"Show each remainder, updated number and any saved original."},
      {title:"Prime testing through the square root",notes:"A prime number greater than 1 has exactly two positive divisors. If n has a factor larger than √n, it must also have a matching factor smaller than √n. Therefore, testing divisors through √n is enough. Handle n<2 separately and stop when the divisor squared exceeds n to avoid floating-point square-root issues.",example:"For 29, test 2, 3, 4 and 5 because 6²>29; none divides, so 29 is prime.",exam:"Explain the paired-factor reason for the square-root limit."},
      {title:"Armstrong numbers and digit powers",notes:"An Armstrong number equals the sum of each digit raised to the number of digits. The common three-digit version uses cubes, but the general definition uses the digit count. Extract each digit, raise it to the required power and accumulate the sum. Keep the original number for the final comparison.",example:"153 has three digits and 1³+5³+3³=1+125+27=153, so it is an Armstrong number.",exam:"State whether the question uses the three-digit cube rule or the general definition."},
      {title:"Factorials, statistics and safe ranges",notes:"Factorial multiplies integers from 1 through n, with 0!=1. Its value grows quickly and can exceed an integer type, so consider the input limit. For a stream of values, maintain count, sum, minimum and maximum; calculate an average only when count is greater than zero. A wider type may be needed for the sum.",example:"For 4, 8 and 12, count=3, sum=24 and average=24/3=8.",exam:"Show initialization, update rule and checks for empty input or overflow."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Function scope and parameter passing",notes:"A function prototype tells the compiler the function name, return type and parameter types. The definition contains the implementation, and a call supplies arguments. In C, ordinary arguments are passed by value, so changing a parameter does not change the caller's variable. A pointer can be passed when a function must modify data owned by the caller.",example:"void addOne(int x) changes only its local copy; addOne(&x) needs a pointer parameter to update the original.",exam:"Distinguish a local parameter value from the caller's variable."},
      {title:"Recursion trace and call stack",notes:"Each recursive call gets its own parameters and local state on the call stack. Trace calls downward until the base case, then trace returned values upward. A valid recursive step must make progress toward the base case. Deep recursion can use substantial stack space, so iteration may be safer for large inputs.",example:"factorial(3) calls factorial(2), then factorial(1), then factorial(0)=1 before returning 1, 2 and 6.",exam:"Show both the stopping case and how each call returns its result."},
      {title:"Array bounds and linear search",notes:"For an array with n elements in C, valid indexes are 0 through n−1. Linear search checks items one by one and works even when values are unsorted. It can stop at the first match or inspect the full array if all matches are needed. Never access index n, and handle an empty array before reading element zero.",example:"In a 5-item array, indexes 0,1,2,3,4 are valid; index 5 is outside the array.",exam:"State the array length and use a loop condition i<n."},
      {title:"Selection sort and matrix dimensions",notes:"Selection sort repeatedly finds the smallest remaining item and swaps it into the next position; its nested comparisons make it O(n²) in typical analysis. Matrix addition is performed element by element and is defined only when both matrices have the same number of rows and columns. Check dimensions before looping.",example:"Add [[1,2],[3,4]] and [[5,6],[7,8]] by adding corresponding cells to get [[6,8],[10,12]].",exam:"Explain the invariant or matching-position rule and verify the dimensions."}
    ]
  ]
};
